{"causes": ["The flourishing of polyphonic music in the late 15th and early 16th centuries created a demand for systematic theoretical instruction.", "The invention of the printing press by Johannes Gutenberg around 1440 enabled wider dissemination of music theory treatises and compositions.", "The Protestant Reformation fostered a need for new liturgical music and pedagogical materials adapted to Lutheran theology and practice."], "effects": ["Galliculus's *Isagoge de compositione cantus* became a standard textbook for music students across the Holy Roman Empire for decades.", "His compositions contributed to the early development of Lutheran church music, particularly in the form of chorale masses and motets.", "He helped transmit and codify the principles of Franco-Flemish polyphony to German-speaking lands, influencing subsequent generations of composers."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "johannes-galliculus", "sourceName": "Johannes Galliculus", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "isagoge-de-compositione-cantus", "targetName": "Isagoge de compositione cantus", "context": "Johannes Galliculus authored the influential music theory treatise *Isagoge de compositione cantus*, first published in 1520."}, {"sourceSlug": "johannes-galliculus", "sourceName": "Johannes Galliculus", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "johann-walter", "targetName": "Johann Walter", "context": "Galliculus's theoretical work and compositional style likely influenced Johann Walter, a foundational composer of early Lutheran church music."}, {"sourceSlug": "johannes-galliculus", "sourceName": "Johannes Galliculus", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "leipzig-holy-roman-empire", "targetName": "Leipzig, Holy Roman Empire", "context": "Johannes Galliculus was active as a musician and theorist primarily in Leipzig during the early 16th century."}, {"sourceSlug": "protestant-reformation", "sourceName": "Protestant Reformation", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "johannes-galliculus", "targetName